  {
    "episode_id": 83468,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83468.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "U.S. policy toward Cuba is about to change. How and to what extent it'll change is less clear. But we know that the White House will issue its review of US Cuba policy in June, most likely in the middle or the end of the month. In the meantime, various bills to keep lifting restrictions against the island are gaining support. Such is a case with the agricultural exports bill, Arkansas Representative Rick Crawford first introduced to Congress in 2015. Crawford has since made some changes to it, after working with congressmen opposed to re-establishing U.S. Cuba ties. I'm Elizabeth Gonzalez from ASCOA Online. And after a year since I last spoke to congressmen Crawford, I circled back to see what this new compromise means for Americans and Cuban Americans and how the mood is changing from Washington to Cuba and more. Thank you so much, Mr. Congressman, for taking the time to date.",
