  {
    "episode_id": 83468,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83468.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Well, basically, it's the same bill that we talked about last year with one added compromise that was at the request of Congressman Carbello. And it is a 2% fee on the transactions that would take place in agricultural sales. And so basically, that 2% would go to liquidating the claims of certified claimants, degrees, Cuban Americans who had lost, had property fees and had certified claims. And so that money would be administered by the Treasury Department. Once it was collected, it would go to liquidate the claims. Burying numbers of, I've heard anywhere from $3 billion in outstanding certified claims to maybe even as high as $8 billion. I'm not sure exactly what the number is, but obviously it would be capped so that it's not a fee that would be going on indefinitely, but once claims are liquidated, then that fee would go away.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
