  {
    "episode_id": 83468,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83468.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, I think some of those things, some of their desires will be reflected in what President Trump announces. From our perspective, I think what what members like Mario Diaz-Billard are concerned about is that we don't do anything that would empower the Castro regime. And so we've obviously taken that into consideration in how we approach these transactions so that we're not seeking to empower the Castro regime. In fact, what we want to do is limit their involvement to the fullest extent that we can, and essentially just allow market access, because if human people have a need for products that we can provide, and it would be a much better value for them, and we can do it without the empowering the regime. But the fact is that You know, the regime military has tentacles and virtually every facet of life in Cuba at this point in time. Although we see, you know, signs that that's changing, and over time we could see some pretty significant reforms. I think that would show that Cuba is on a positive trajectory toward economic and social and political change. And so, you know, My perspective is that we should play a role there",
