    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "to shape that and steer that in a productive way as opposed to just kind of waiting on the sidelines until all the conditions are right. Because all the conditions are not going to be right. And we trade with nations around the world where the conditions are not right, but we do it anyway. And there are nations around the world that we trade with daily that have pretty egregious human rights violations and currency manipulations and you know free speech issues and religious freedom issues and things of that nature that don't necessarily square with American values and yet we continue to trade with nations like that but the exception being Cuba. Now we recognize that Cuba is not a nation that is aligned with us but you know the history of Cuba is that there is a familial bond there and that is evidenced by the amount of US dollars of remittances that go back to Cuba to assist family members of Cuban Americans that are still in Cuba. And so that and the fact that they are in such close proximity, 90 miles off of the tip of Florida, it's certainly in our interest to review what our history has been over the last 55 years and take a more positive role. And I think this is a simple first step in that direction. to make incremental progress and show the Cuban people, not the regime, but the Cuban people that we are interested in providing some level of support for everyday Cubans and helping them meet basic nutrition needs.",
