  {
    "episode_id": 83468,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83468.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "I think so, and I think one of the things that I don't live in South Florida, but I've seen articles and from different publications where you have a generational shift in attitude toward Cuba and that you have younger folks, and I hate to use the term millennial, but that seems to be the term to describe younger folks in that age category. That are sort of questioning, okay, now tell me again why we're not allowed to go to Cuba and why we don't do business in Cuba. And look, it doesn't change history, but it's just a different perspective because You know, you're talking about a generation of young people that were not a party to that and didn't, you know, don't necessarily understand it in any other way other than sort of the abstract concept that's been communicated to them from their parents and grandparents. And I think they have some questions and in many cases, it's certainly legitimate for us to be able to have that conversation. You know, I'm not a Cuban American, so I can't necessarily relate personally to the feelings that Cuban Americans have with regard to Cuba. But what I can say is that we've made every effort to respect that and to understand that the entire nation also has an opinion and that should be respectful of those folks who have lived through that and who fled the regime and who were mistreated and abused and all the stories that go along with that. But at the same time recognizing that over the past 55 years, our policy toward Cuba has really done nothing to benefit the United States or even those Cuban Americans that were mistreated. Instead, it's really had the reverse effect of being able to be used by a Castro regime as a propaganda tool to help undermine the United States and also used as an excuse When things go bad in Cuba, they can just point to the embargo and blame the Americans. And so it's a propaganda tool. And I think all of these kinds of things are now sort of becoming more apparent to a next generation of Cuban Americans who are asking those questions about. Okay, why are we not allowed to go back and visit Cuba? Why are we not engaged economically in Cuba?",
